    <track>
      <title>When fate deals its mortal blow [Australia, blues punk] (1983)</title>
      <creator>The Scientists</creator>
      <image>https://vintageobscura.net/yt_img/7QOzdNiyWNI.jpg</image>
      <album>Recent Discoveries</album>
    </track>
    <track>
      <title>MacArthur Park [USA, School, Psyche, Pop] (1971) : Really ambitious for a High School band to cover this epic Jimmy Webb song famously sung by Richard Harris. This version is comparatively stripped down and really reveals its hypnotic and psychedelic qualities.</title>
      <creator>The Davison High School Jazz Band</creator>
      <image>https://vintageobscura.net/yt_img/4gHeKnrXdpY.jpg</image>
      <album>Psychedelia</album>
    </track>
    <track>
      <title>Psych Jazz, Prog, Chanson] (1972)</title>
      <creator>Brégent ‎- Dans Ma Maison [Canada</creator>
      <image>https://vintageobscura.net/yt_img/nxQ3eLoW7Os.jpg</image>
      <album>Psychedelia</album>
    </track>
    <track>
      <title>Hurt To See You Go [USA, Psyche, Folk Rock] (196-) Who was Jimmy Brown? Whoever he was, what is certain is that he created one of the best psyche tinged, baroque folk-rock records I have ever heard. The sheer talent and ambition that saturates this four track E.P. is mind blowing.</title>
      <creator>Jimmy Brown</creator>
      <image>https://vintageobscura.net/yt_img/s3VrVg8rS8w.jpg</image>
      <album>Psychedelia</album>
    </track>
  </trackList>
